I've characterized HPL (icl-utk-edu/hpl) as a single-variant application:

**Subject:** `xhpl` — the High Performance Linpack benchmark, built from `testing/ptest/HPL_pddriver.c` as the single executable target (no GPU forks, no CMake — classic autotools/`Make.<arch>` build producing `bin/<arch>/xhpl`).

**Key findings:**
- **Parallelism:** Pure MPI (`MPI_Init` in the driver, `MPI_Comm_rank/size`), no OpenMP/CUDA in the HPL source itself — though linked BLAS libraries (e.g., threaded MKL) can add per-rank threading as a build-time choice.
- **Communication:** Mixed model — custom point-to-point ring/spread/roll implementations (`HPL_1ring.c`, `HPL_2ring.c`, `HPL_rollN.c`, `HPL_spreadT.c`) for panel broadcasts down the 2D process grid (`HPL_grid_init` splits into row/col communicators), plus classic reductions (`HPL_all_reduce`, max/sum) for machine-epsilon and residual checks. Both latency-sensitive (pivot/row-swap exchanges) and bandwidth-sensitive (panel broadcasts).
- **No accelerator, no shared-memory windows, no parallel I/O, no checkpoint/restart** — confirmed by absence of CUDA/HIP/MPI_Win/shm_open/HDF5/MPI_File in source.
- **Launch geometry:** Only concrete invocation found is `mpirun -np 4 xhpl` in INSTALL; actual rank count is determined by user's HPL.dat `P x Q` grid parameters — genuinely variable, so recorded with only that documented example and marked medium confidence.
- **Topology:** judged "either" span (runs single-node for small grids but is the canonical multi-node Top500 cluster benchmark), with neighbor-style placement preference due to the row/column communicator structure.

Both `record_shape` calls were recorded under `(xhpl, general)` since this repository builds only one real variant of the application.
